      "chapter": "General Information",
      "table_no": null,
      "title": "[The chief lakes of China Proper]",
      "caption_extra": "Section: Geography. Lakes. \"The chief lakes of China Proper are:—\"",
      "parts": [
        {
          "label": null,
          "columns": [
            "No.",
            "Lake",
            "Description"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1.",
              "Tungting",
              "The Tungting lake in Hunan. In summer, when it receives the overflow of the Yangtze, its dimensions reach 75 miles in length and 56 in breadth (2000 sq. miles); but in winter the lake empties its waters into the Yangtze and becomes merely an enormous marsh, with two main river channels passing through it, the Yuenkiang and Siangkiang. In 1910 the provincial authorities made a start of dredging the lake, a machine having been bought for that purpose in Germany. The dredger was manned and operated by a Chinese crew, and began work on the Changteh channel."
            ],
            [
              "2.",
              "Po-yang",
              "The Po-yang lake in Kiangsi; 90 miles by 20 (approx.) The water rises nearly 30 ft. in the flood season, when the area of the lake is approximately 1800 sq. miles."
            ],
            [
              "3.",
              "Tai-hu",
              "The Tai-hu, or Great Lake, in Kiangsu, S. of the Yangtze. Its greatest dimensions are approximately 50 miles by 40 miles, but its waters recede in winter."
            ],
            [
              "4.",
              "Hungtseh",
              "The Hungtseh lake in Kiangsu and Anhwei, N. of the Yangtze, connected on the N.E. with the Grand Canal and on the S.E. with"
            ],
            [
              "5.",
              "Kaoyiu",
              "The Kaoyiu lake, also in Kiangsu."
            ],
            [
              "6.",
              "Tatsung",
              "The Tatsung lake lies E. of the Kaoyin, on the other side of the Grand Canal."
            ],
            [
              "7.",
              "Tien-hu",
              "The Tien-hu lake in Yunnan, S. of Yunnanfu: approximately 38 miles in length and 8 in width and lying at an altitude of 6300 ft."
            ],
            [
              "8.",
              "Eulhai",
              "The Eulhai lake, also in Yunnan, E. of Talifu. It lies at an altitude of 6500 ft. above sea-level, but is slightly smaller than the Tien-hu."
            ],
            [
              "9.",
              "Dalai-nor; Buir-nor, or Pir Lake; Lake Hinka (Khanka)",
              "In Manchuria are to be found the Dalai-nor and Buir-nor, or Pir Lake, both S.W. of Hailar, and Lake Hinka (Khanka) in the extreme east of the province, W. of the Ussuri River."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "transcriber_notes": [
